INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE TESTING CHAT.  Pass on everything from this line down
to the diffstat.

Goal: run tools/testing/selftests/mm/memory-failure with its working
directory on every filesystem an x86_64 QEMU guest can mount, without and
with the complete patch stack, and report one table.

These patches change only the selftest.  So:

- Build ONE kernel, with every filesystem below built in (=y, not =m).
  Do not build a kernel per filesystem.  The same kernel runs both test
  binaries.
- Build the selftest twice: binary A from the tree without this series,
  binary B with the complete patch stack.  Run them directly as root,
  with the current directory on the filesystem under test.  Do not use
  run_vmtests.sh: it does not change directory.

Kernel: current mm-new, x86_64 defconfig, plus

  the test needs   MEMORY_FAILURE HWPOISON_INJECT DEBUG_FS
                   PROC_PAGE_MONITOR BLK_DEV_LOOP
  disk             EXT2_FS EXT4_FS XFS_FS BTRFS_FS F2FS_FS JFS_FS
                   NILFS2_FS GFS2_FS OCFS2_FS EXFAT_FS VFAT_FS NTFS3_FS
                   NTFS_FS UDF_FS MINIX_FS HFSPLUS_FS
  network          NFS_FS NFS_V3 NFS_V4 NFS_V4_2 NFSD NFSD_V4 CIFS
                   SMB_SERVER 9P_FS NET_9P_VIRTIO
  FUSE, stacked    FUSE_FS VIRTIO_FS OVERLAY_FS ECRYPT_FS
  memory only      TMPFS HUGETLBFS

Check .config afterwards: every symbol above must be =y.  If one is not,
find the missing dependency; do not drop it silently.

In the guest, mount debugfs on /sys/kernel/debug.  Then for each line
below make the filesystem, mount it, cd into a fresh directory on it, and
run A then B.

  ext2 ext4 xfs btrfs f2fs jfs nilfs2 exfat vfat udf minix hfsplus
               one loop image each, 1G
  ntfs3, ntfs  the same mkntfs image, mounted once with each driver
  gfs2         mkfs.gfs2 -p lock_nolock -j 1
  ocfs2        mkfs.ocfs2 -M local
  nfs v3, nfs v4.2
               loopback mount of a knfsd export (rw,no_root_squash) of
               the ext4 mount
  cifs         loopback mount of a ksmbd or samba share of the ext4 mount
  9p           QEMU -virtfs share; run with cache=mmap, again with
               cache=loose
  virtiofs     virtiofsd on the host; run with its default cache mode
               and again with writeback enabled
  fuse         a libfuse passthrough example over the ext4 mount
  overlayfs    upper and lower on ext4; again with upper and lower on
               virtiofs if it will mount
  ecryptfs     over the ext4 mount
  tmpfs, ramfs controls
  hugetlbfs    control; first echo 8 > /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages

On fuse, virtiofs and anything else where the kernel logs "Failed to
invalidate", run B 20 times, not once, and report how many runs passed.
The failure being looked for is in teardown:

  memory-failure.c:211:...:Expected unpoison_memory(self->pfn) (-16) == 0

For each run record the "# Totals:" line, every "not ok" line with the
"Expected ..." line above it, and the kernel's "Memory failure: ...
recovery action for dirty LRU page: ..." lines.

Expected with B: pass:10 fail:0 skip:0 on backed filesystems other than
NILFS2 and CephFS; pass:9 skip:1 on each of those; pass:6 skip:4 on
tmpfs, ramfs and hugetlbfs.
Expected with A: fail:1 where close() reports the error, skip:1 on NFS,
3 failures on ramfs, 4 on hugetlbfs.

Rules:

- A failure is a result.  Report it.  Do not change the selftest, the
  kernel, the mount options or the list to make something pass.
- If a filesystem cannot be made or mounted, report it as NOT RUN with the
  exact command and error.  Do not leave it out.
- If B fails, say which assertion: fsync() returning 0 means that
  filesystem does not report the error from fsync(); close() failing
  after fsync() means the error was not consumed; ftruncate() failing
  means the poisoned page could not be removed.  All are findings.
- If B fails on a filesystem at fsync() and that filesystem has no backing
  store, say so: it probably belongs in fs_has_backing_store().
- Check HardwareCorrupted in /proc/meminfo before the first run and after
  the last.  It must be unchanged.

Report one table with a row per run above: filesystem and mount options,
A totals, B totals (and passes out of 20 where repeated), first failing
assertion, recovery action (Recovered, Failed or Delayed) for the hard
dirty_pagecache test.  Then list anything NOT RUN.

 tools/testing/selftests/mm/memory-failure.c |   38 ++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/tools/testing/selftests/mm/memory-failure.c~selftests-mm-check-that-fsync-reports-hard-poisoned-dirty-page-cache
+++ a/tools/testing/selftests/mm/memory-failure.c
@@ -300,6 +300,29 @@ static int get_fs_type(int fd)
 	return ret ? 0 : (int)fs.f_type;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The page-cache tests need a filesystem which keeps its data on backing
+ * store.  When a clean page is poisoned the kernel drops it and the data is
+ * read back in.  When a dirty page is poisoned the data is lost, and the
+ * kernel records -EIO in the mapping for fsync() to report.
+ *
+ * tmpfs and ramfs keep their data only in the page cache.  The poisoned
+ * page cannot be dropped and read back, and their fsync() does nothing, so
+ * neither expectation holds.  hugetlbfs is the same, and it also poisons a
+ * whole huge page.  shmem has its own tests below.
+ */
+static bool fs_has_backing_store(int fs_type)
+{
+	switch ((unsigned int)fs_type) {
+	case 0:
+	case TMPFS_MAGIC:
+	case RAMFS_MAGIC:
+	case HUGETLBFS_MAGIC:
+		return false;
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
 TEST_F(memory_failure, clean_pagecache)
 {
 	int fd;
@@ -311,7 +334,7 @@ TEST_F(memory_failure, clean_pagecache)
 	if (fd < 0)
 		SKIP(return, "failed to open test file.\n");
 	fs_type = get_fs_type(fd);
-	if (!fs_type || fs_type == TMPFS_MAGIC) {
+	if (!fs_has_backing_store(fs_type)) {
 		close(fd);
 		SKIP(return, "unsupported filesystem :%x\n", fs_type);
 	}
@@ -353,13 +376,7 @@ TEST_F(memory_failure, dirty_pagecache)
 	if (fd < 0)
 		SKIP(return, "failed to open test file.\n");
 	fs_type = get_fs_type(fd);
-	/*
-	 * MADV_HARD poisoning of dirty page-cache data records an expected
-	 * -EIO in the file mapping. NFS reports this error on close(), so
-	 * skip this variant.
-	 */
-	if (!fs_type || fs_type == TMPFS_MAGIC ||
-	    (fs_type == NFS_SUPER_MAGIC && variant->type == MADV_HARD)) {
+	if (!fs_has_backing_store(fs_type)) {
 		close(fd);
 		SKIP(return, "unsupported filesystem :%x\n", fs_type);
 	}
@@ -386,6 +403,11 @@ TEST_F(memory_failure, dirty_pagecache)
 
 	ASSERT_EQ(munmap(addr, self->page_size), 0);
 
+	if (variant->type == MADV_HARD) {
+		ASSERT_EQ(fsync(fd), -1);
+		ASSERT_EQ(errno, EIO);
+	}
+
 	ASSERT_EQ(close(fd), 0);
 }
 
